From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: mt6363: Always report overcurrent events

The overcurrent interrupt handler only calls the notifier if the enable bit
of the regulator is set. If the hardware reports an error, the error should
be reported, as Mark Brown pointed out on the MT6373 driver, which has the
same code. MediaTek's driver has this check too, and does not say why.

Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/agKKuH0fP3lBMuFd@sirena.co.uk
Fixes: 3c36965df808 ("regulator: Add support for MediaTek MT6363 SPMI PMIC Regulators")
---
 drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c | 3 +--
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c b/drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c
index 76dbead..03a9169 100644
--- a/drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/mt6363-regulator.c
@@ -630,8 +630,7 @@ static irqreturn_t mt6363_oc_isr(int irq, void *data)
 
 	disable_irq_nosync(info->virq);
 
-	if (regulator_is_enabled_regmap(rdev))
-		regulator_notifier_call_chain(rdev, REGULATOR_EVENT_OVER_CURRENT, NULL);
+	regulator_notifier_call_chain(rdev, REGULATOR_EVENT_OVER_CURRENT, NULL);
 
 	schedule_delayed_work(&info->oc_work, msecs_to_jiffies(OC_IRQ_ENABLE_DELAY_MS));
 
